ot, service, files, permissions, enablement, probes, and reboot all had clear roles. One bounded unnecessary intermediate mutation occurred: cmd-6a03812c01644e0d edited the live init script to load an unavailable syscall.ph and restarted the service, temporarily leaving it stopped. It was fully corrected by cmd-7442ef012b3c474a, which restored service operation and retained only the working supplementary-group clearing implementation. No new temporary-file residue or listening sockets were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6a03812c01644e0d","cmd-7442ef012b3c474a","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct pre- and post-reboot evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged, the boot identity changed, and the service, heartbeat data, identity, confinement, permissions, and enablement recovered. Fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the boot transition.","limitations":["The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation and therefore does not independently establish package consistency; its identical before/after output shows no executor-relative regression but is not a substantive health check.","Writable-location verification was strong but bounded to Unix ownership/mode scanning and explicit probes; no separate ACL inventory was captured."]}
